From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-controller 99994: persist under the node the TLV named, not the relay

99992 taught handle_topology_response() to believe the Device Information TLV
over the frame source. It works - the live model of the far node is correct -
but only the live model. The caller keeps its own copy of the node pointer, and
that is the one every mapc_sync_*() call in cntlr_handle_map_event() uses:

	n = cntlr_find_node(c, origin);		/* the relay */
	...
	ret = f[idx].handle(c, cmdu, n);	/* handler corrects its own n */
	...
	mapc_sync_agent(c, n);			/* still the relay */
	mapc_sync_radio(c, n, r);
	mapc_sync_apmld(c, n);
	mapc_sync_bstamld(c, n);

So a Topology Response from a two-hop node updates that node in memory and then
writes the relay to the database - and the relay's rows are rebuilt from its
own, correct model, so nothing looks wrong. The far node simply never gets
written and keeps whatever it had.

Measured on hardware 2026-08-04, mesh chained as controller <- 8g <- x8, with
99992 already deployed:

	iw:	x8 -> ca:8e:1d:ca:bc:af (8g),  8g -> 86:f7:56:41:01:d4 (4g)

	agent		x8 -> 86:f7:56:41:01:d4  depth 1   <- yesterday's value
			8g -> 86:f7:56:41:01:d4  depth 1   correct
	topology_link	correct for both		   (built elsewhere)
	apmld		4 rows: 4g and 8g only, x8 absent
	bss		x8 has 4 of its 5
	bstamld		06:0c:43:26:81:10 -> ca:8e:1d:ca:bc:ae

That last row is the whole defect in one line: 06:0c:43:26:81:10 is the bSTA MLD
of x8, filed under the AL MAC of 8g. Both agents reported correctly over ubus
the entire time.

Which rows survive is decided by list order in i1905_link_lookup_partial(),
because ieee1905 holds two links for the relay's address - one to the relay,
one to the node behind it. That is why x8 has radio rows but not apmld rows:
some responses were attributed correctly by luck and some were not.

Let a handler that has identified the true author say so by rewriting
cmdu->origin, and re-resolve the node from it once the handler returns, before
anything is persisted. handle_topology_response() is the only handler that does
this today; the rest fall through unchanged, because cmdu->origin still holds
what the caller put there.

Also refresh last_tsp_seen on the author. Node expiry runs off that timestamp,
and until now a node two hops out only had it refreshed when the origin
happened to resolve its way.
--- a/src/cntlr_map.c
+++ b/src/cntlr_map.c
@@ -456,6 +456,13 @@
 				    MACFMT ", using that\n", __func__,
 				    MAC2STR(n->almacaddr), MAC2STR(di->aladdr));
 				n = real;
+
+				/* Correcting our own copy is not enough. The
+				 * caller persists the node *it* resolved, not
+				 * the one we settled on, so say it somewhere
+				 * the caller reads back.
+				 */
+				memcpy(cmdu->origin, real->almacaddr, 6);
 			}
 		}
 	}
@@ -5160,8 +5167,33 @@
 		cntlr_notify_plugins(c, cmdu);
 	}
 
-	if (!n)
-		n = cntlr_find_node(c, origin);
+	/* A handler may have found that `origin` was only the relay and left the
+	 * real author in cmdu->origin. Everything below here writes to the
+	 * database, so resolve it once, in one place, instead of in every
+	 * mapc_sync_*() call.
+	 *
+	 * When no handler touched it, cmdu->origin still holds `origin` and this
+	 * lookup returns the same node - including the case where the node did
+	 * not exist until the handler created it, which is what the `if (!n)`
+	 * this replaces was for.
+	 */
+	{
+		struct node *author = cntlr_find_node(c, cmdu->origin);
+
+		if (author && author != n) {
+			dbg("%s: cmdu %04x is from " MACFMT ", relayed by " MACFMT
+			    "; persisting under the former\n", __func__, cmdutype,
+			    MAC2STR(author->almacaddr), MAC2STR(origin));
+
+			/* Node expiry runs off this timestamp. A node two hops
+			 * out only ever had it refreshed when the origin
+			 * happened to resolve its way. */
+			timestamp_update(&author->last_tsp_seen);
+		}
+
+		if (author)
+			n = author;
+	}
 
 	if (n)
 		timestamp_update(&n->last_cmdu);
